= 1
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <classification of compact surfaces> says that every <compact> connected <surface> without boundary is <homeomorphic> to exactly one of the following: the <sphere>, an <orientable surface> obtained as the <connected sum> of $g\geq1$ <tori>, or a <nonorientable surface> obtained as the <connected sum> of $k\geq1$ real <projective planes>. The integers $g$ and $k$ are unique, and the orientable and nonorientable families do not overlap. Including $g=0$ makes the <sphere> the genus-zero member of the orientable family.

Their <Euler characteristics> are $2-2g$ and $2-k$, respectively. Thus **<orientability> and the <Euler characteristic> determine the closed connected <surface>**. If boundaries are allowed, one additionally specifies the number of boundary circles and removes that many open discs from a closed model.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a finite polygonal <CW complex> decomposition of a <compact> <surface>, with $V$ vertices, $E$ edges and $F$ faces after all identifications,
$$
\boxed{\chi=V-E+F.}
$$
This is the <Euler characteristic>, independent of the chosen subdivision. In a polygonal presentation, count <equivalence classes> of vertices and paired edges, rather than their number before gluing.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use a square with boundary word $aba^{-1}b^{-1}$, identifying opposite sides with matching labels and arrows. All four vertices become one, the four sides form two edge classes, and there is one face. Therefore the <torus> has
$$
\boxed{\chi(T)=1-2+1=0.}
$$

= b
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A polygonal model of the real <projective plane> is a bigon with boundary word $aa$, with the equally labelled arrows identified. Its two vertices become one, its sides form one edge, and it has one face. Thus
$$
\boxed{\chi(P)=1-1+1=1.}
$$
Equivalently, glue antipodal boundary points of a disc.

= c
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A square with boundary word $aba^{-1}b$ gives the <Klein bottle>. The side identifications produce one vertex, two edges and one face. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\chi(K)=1-2+1=0.}
$$
Unlike the <torus>, this <surface> is nonorientable; its equal <Euler characteristic> alone does not distinguish them.

= d
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Glue two triangular discs along their boundaries to obtain a <sphere>. The resulting <CW complex> decomposition has three vertices, three edges and two faces, giving
$$
\boxed{\chi(S^2)=3-3+2=2.}
$$
The two triangles represent the two closed hemispheres, with an equator subdivided into three edges.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For $g\geq1$, the standard commutator-word polygon has one face, $2g$ paired edge classes and a single vertex class. Thus
$$
\boxed{\chi(X)=1-2g+1=2-2g.}
$$
An <orientable surface> admits continuously compatible local orientations; for a smooth <surface>, this is equivalently a continuous choice of <unit normal vector> in an oriented ambient three-dimensional space when such an embedding is available. In a polygonal model, orientability means the faces can be oriented so that induced boundary directions on each glued edge oppose one another. Here each edge label occurs once positively and once negatively. The orientation of the polygon therefore descends consistently across every seam. Hence **$X$ is orientable**, and it is the <closed orientable surface> of genus $g$.

= 2
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a regular <parametric surface> $r(u,v)$, set
$$
E=r_u\cdot r_u,\quad F=r_u\cdot r_v,\quad G=r_v\cdot r_v,\qquad N=\frac{r_u\times r_v}{\|r_u\times r_v\|}.
$$
The <first fundamental form> is the induced <Riemannian metric>
$$
\mathrm I=E\,du^2+2F\,du\,dv+G\,dv^2.
$$
The <second fundamental form>, for this choice of <unit normal vector>, is
$$
\mathrm{II}=e\,du^2+2f_2\,du\,dv+g_2\,dv^2,\quad e=r_{uu}\cdot N,\quad f_2=r_{uv}\cdot N,\quad g_2=r_{vv}\cdot N.
$$
The <Gaussian curvature> is the product of the <principal curvatures>, equivalently the <determinant> of the <shape operator>:
$$
\boxed{K=\frac{eg_2-f_2^2}{EG-F^2}.}
$$
Reversing the normal reverses the <second fundamental form> and both <principal curvatures>, while leaving $K$ unchanged.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Work on a regular patch with $f(u)>0$. Differentiating the <surface of revolution> gives
$$
r_u=(f'\cos v,f'\sin v,h'),\qquad r_v=(-f\sin v,f\cos v,0).
$$
The arc-length condition gives $E=1$, $F=0$, $G=f^2$. Also $\|r_u\times r_v\|=f$, so take
$$
N=(-h'\cos v,-h'\sin v,f').
$$
The second derivatives yield
$$
e=f'h''-f''h',\qquad f_2=0,\qquad g_2=fh'.
$$
Thus the <first fundamental form> and <second fundamental form> are
$$
\boxed{\mathrm I=du^2+f^2dv^2,\qquad\mathrm{II}=(f'h''-f''h')du^2+fh'\,dv^2.}
$$
The opposite normal reverses the second displayed form. At a smooth pole the revolution coordinates degenerate, so <curvature> is understood by a regular local chart or by continuity.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Differentiating the arc-length identity gives $f'f''+h'h''=0$. Multiply by $f'$ and use $(f')^2+(h')^2=1$ to obtain
$$
(f'h''-f''h')h'=f'h'h''-f''(h')^2=-f''\bigl((f')^2+(h')^2\bigr)=-f''.
$$
This derivation does not divide by $h'$, so it remains valid where $h'=0$. Inserting the <first fundamental form> and <second fundamental form> computed above gives the <Gaussian curvature of a surface of revolution>:
$$
\boxed{K=\frac{(f'h''-f''h')fh'}{f^2}=-\frac{f''}{f}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Choose $f(u)=\sin u$ and $h(u)=\cos u$ for $0\leq u\leq\pi$. Adding the smooth poles at the endpoints gives the unit <sphere>. Its meridian is parametrized by <arc length>, and $-f''/f=1$ away from the poles, hence everywhere by continuity. Thus **the unit <sphere> has constant <Gaussian curvature> one**.

There is **no <compact> smooth <surface of revolution> without boundary of constant <Gaussian curvature> minus one** in this setting. On a <compact> such <surface>, the distance $f$ from the rotation axis attains a positive maximum. The maximum lies on a regular revolution patch, where the meridian <arc length> coordinate gives $f''\leq0$. Hence $K=-f''/f\geq0$ there, contradicting $K=-1$. Equivalently, constant negative <curvature> would require $f''=f>0$, excluding any positive radial maximum. Compact patches with boundary would not be excluded by this argument, but they are not closed surfaces.

= 3
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For an affinely parametrized <geodesic> $s\mapsto(x(s),y(s))$, the energy <Lagrangian> is $L=(\dot x^2+\dot y^2)/(2y^2)$. The coordinate $x$ is cyclic, so the <Euler-Lagrange equation> gives $\dot x/y^2=c$, a constant. Parametrize a nonconstant <geodesic> by hyperbolic <arc length>; then
$$
\dot x=cy^2,\qquad\dot x^2+\dot y^2=y^2.
$$
If $c=0$, $x$ is constant: these are vertical lines. If $c\ne0$, on intervals with $\dot y\ne0$,
$$
\frac{dx}{dy}=\frac{\pm cy}{\sqrt{1-c^2y^2}}.
$$
Integration gives $(x-a)^2+y^2=c^{-2}$. The two branches join smoothly at the highest point. Conversely these curves satisfy the <geodesic equations>, or are images of vertical lines under half-plane <Möbius transformations> that are <isometries>. Thus the complete nonconstant <geodesics> are
$$
\boxed{x=\text{constant},\qquad(x-a)^2+y^2=R^2\ (y>0),}
$$
namely vertical lines and upper semicircles orthogonal to the real axis. Their subarcs give <geodesic segments>.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Cayley transform> $w=(z-i)/(z+i)$ maps the upper half-plane conformally onto the unit disc. Its inverse is $z=i(1+w)/(1-w)$, with
$$
|dz|^2=\frac{4|dw|^2}{|1-w|^4},\qquad\operatorname{Im}z=\frac{1-|w|^2}{|1-w|^2}.
$$
Transporting the given <hyperbolic metric> therefore gives the <Poincare disc model>
$$
\boxed{ds_D^2=\frac{4|dw|^2}{(1-|w|^2)^2}.}
$$
The <Cayley transform> is an <isometry> for this metric, so it carries the half-plane <geodesics> to disc <geodesics>. A <Möbius transformation> sends generalized circles to generalized circles and preserves angles. Hence these are exactly **diameters and arcs of circles meeting the unit circle orthogonally**. Multiplying this <Riemannian metric> by any positive constant leaves the unparametrized <geodesics> unchanged.

\Image[2010-hyperbolic-geodesics.png]
{title=Half-plane geodesics and their Cayley images in the disc}

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For distinct points $a,b$ on a <geodesic>, the given disc <isometry> $z\mapsto e^{i\theta}(z-a)/(1-\bar az)$ sends $a$ to zero. Choose $\theta$ so that the image of $b$ is the positive number
$$
x=\left|\frac{b-a}{1-\bar ab}\right|\in(0,1).
$$
An orthogonal-circle <geodesic> through zero is a diameter, so this normalizes the <geodesic> as required.

There are two <hyperbolic metric> normalizations to distinguish. For the metric transferred from the stated half-plane, the radial length is
$$
d_D(0,x)=\int_0^x\frac{2\,dt}{1-t^2}=2\operatorname{artanh}x.
$$
The diameter is minimizing. Indeed, any path $w(t)$ from zero to $x$ has $|\dot w|\geq|d|w|/dt|$, so its length is at least $2\operatorname{artanh}x$, with equality on the radial segment. Therefore the distance consistent with the given half-plane metric is
$$
\boxed{d_D(a,b)=2\operatorname{artanh}\left|\frac{b-a}{1-\bar ab}\right|.}
$$
For the alternative disc metric $d\widetilde s_D^2=|dw|^2/(1-|w|^2)^2$, every length is halved and the same isometries and <geodesics> remain. In that convention,
$$
\boxed{\widetilde d_D(a,b)=\operatorname{artanh}\left|\frac{b-a}{1-\bar ab}\right|.}
$$
**The printed distance corresponds to the latter convention.** It is obtained from one quarter of the given half-plane metric, rather than its direct transfer. The equal-point case gives distance zero in both conventions. This is the <normalization of the Poincare disc metric>.

