= 4
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

First consider the unlettered <projective frame> assertion. A list of $n+2$ points in $\mathbb{CP}^n$ is in general position when every $n+1$ of their representative vectors are <linearly independent>, equivalently no $n+1$ of the points lie in a projective hyperplane. Choose representatives $v_1,\ldots,v_{n+1}$ of the first $n+1$ points as a <basis>. Write $v_{n+2}=\sum c_iv_i$. Each $c_i$ is nonzero, since otherwise $v_{n+2}$ and the other $n$ basis vectors would be dependent. Rescaling the representatives makes $v_{n+2}=v_1+\cdots+v_{n+1}$.

Do the same for the second frame with representatives $w_i$. The <invertible linear map> $v_i\mapsto w_i$ induces a <projective transformation> carrying all $n+2$ points as required. A transformation fixing a normalized frame is represented by a diagonal matrix, since it fixes the first $n+1$ points. Fixing the sum point forces every diagonal entry to coincide, so the matrix is scalar and the <projective transformation> is the identity. This proves **existence and uniqueness**.

Return to the four chosen points on the two lines. Since the six points are distinct, at most one is $K\cap L$; put its index third if necessary. No three of $p_1,p_2,q_1,q_2$ are collinear: every triple contains two on one line and a point on the other line away from the intersection. Thus they form a <projective frame> in $\mathbb{CP}^2$. The four target points also have no collinear triple, as their three-by-three <determinants> are nonzero. The frame result gives **a unique <projective transformation> with the prescribed four images**.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The normalized lines are $K=\{z=0\}$ and $L=\{y=0\}$. A point of $K$ with first coordinate zero is $p_1$, so $p_3\ne p_1$ permits unique normalization $p_3=[1,\alpha,0]$. The inequality $p_3\ne p_2$ gives $\alpha\ne1$. Similarly, $q_3=[1,0,\beta]$ uniquely with $\beta\ne1$.

The lines $p_1q_2$ and $p_2q_1$ are $x-z=0$ and $x-y=0$, giving $r_{12}=[1,1,1]$. The lines $p_1q_3$ and $p_3q_1$ are $\beta x-z=0$ and $\alpha x-y=0$, giving $r_{13}=[1,\alpha,\beta]$. Finally, the two lines defining $r_{23}$ have equations
$$
\beta x-\beta y-z=0,\qquad\alpha x-y-\alpha z=0.
$$
Taking the <cross product> of their coefficient vectors gives
$$
\boxed{r_{12}=[1,1,1],\quad r_{13}=[1,\alpha,\beta],\quad r_{23}=[\alpha\beta-1,\alpha(\beta-1),\beta(\alpha-1)].}
$$
The last vector cannot vanish: if $\alpha\beta=1$, both parameters are nonzero and vanishing of its other coordinates would force $\alpha=\beta=1$, which is excluded.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $u=(1,1,1)$ and $v=(1,\alpha,\beta)$. The vector representing $r_{23}$ satisfies
$$
(\alpha\beta-1,\alpha(\beta-1),\beta(\alpha-1))=\alpha\beta\,u-v.
$$
Consequently the three representative vectors are <linearly dependent>, so **$r_{12},r_{13},r_{23}$ are collinear** in the <projective plane>. Equivalently their three-by-three <determinant> vanishes. This is the two-line configuration of <Pappus's hexagon theorem>.

= 5
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The strong <Bézout theorem> says that if two projective plane <algebraic curves> $C,D$ over $\mathbb C$ have degrees $m,n$ and no common irreducible component, their intersection is finite and
$$
\boxed{\sum_{p\in C\cap D}I_p(C,D)=mn.}
$$
Each <intersection multiplicity> is a positive integer. Scheme-theoretic multiplicities, rather than just distinct intersection points, are essential: tangencies and singularities can make individual terms larger than one.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For curves with no common local component at $p$,
$$
\boxed{I_p(C,D)=1\iff C,D\text{ are both smooth at }p\text{ and have distinct tangent lines}.}
$$
In local affine coordinates, their defining equations have linearly independent gradients exactly in this case. These independent linear terms form local coordinates, so the local intersection quotient has <dimension> one. Conversely, if either equation lacks a nonzero linear term or the two linear terms are dependent, the local intersection quotient cannot have <dimension> one. This is <transverse intersection of plane curves> of two smooth plane curves.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write the irreducible curve as $F=0$, with $F$ homogeneous of degree $n$. For $n=1$ there are no singular points. Otherwise choose a nonzero partial derivative, say $F_x$, which has degree $n-1$. Such a derivative exists in <characteristic zero>. Irreducibility and its smaller degree show that $F$ and $F_x$ have no common component.

At every <singular point> $p$ of $C$, $F_x(p)=0$ and the curve multiplicity of $F$ is at least two. The standard local lower bound for <intersection multiplicity> gives $I_p(F,F_x)\geq2$. Applying the <Bézout theorem>,
$$
2\#\operatorname{Sing}(C)\leq\sum_{p\in C\cap\{F_x=0\}}I_p(F,F_x)=n(n-1).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\#\operatorname{Sing}(C)\leq\tfrac12n(n-1).}
$$
This is the <polar bound for singular points of a plane curve>.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Take the union of $n$ distinct projective lines in general position, with no three concurrent. Its defining polynomial is the product of their linear equations, so its degree is $n$. Exactly two lines meet at each of the $\binom n2$ pairwise intersections, producing an ordinary double point; elsewhere just one line passes through and the curve is smooth. Hence
$$
\boxed{\#\operatorname{Sing}(C)=\binom n2=\tfrac12n(n-1).}
$$
For $n=1$, the line itself gives zero singular points, as required.

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The curves have no common component, since their intersection is finite. Let $R=0$ define the irreducible degree-$m$ curve $E$. Choose $p\in E$ outside $C\cap D$, and select $(\lambda,\mu)\ne(0,0)$ with $\lambda P(p)+\mu Q(p)=0$. The polynomial $H=\lambda P+\mu Q$ is not identically zero, since $P,Q$ are not proportional.

The degree-$n$ curve $H=0$ meets $E$ at the prescribed $nm$ base points and also at $p$. If $R$ did not divide $H$, irreducibility of $R$ and the <Bézout theorem> would bound the number of distinct intersections by $nm$, contradicting these $nm+1$ points. Thus
$$
H=RS,\qquad\deg S=n-m.
$$
At every remaining common zero of $P,Q$ lying off $E$, $R$ is nonzero and hence $S=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{F=\{S=0\}\text{ contains all remaining }n(n-m)\text{ points},\qquad\deg F\leq n-m.}
$$
This is <residual intersection through a curve pencil>. The existence of a point $p$ outside the finite base locus is automatic on the projective curve $E$.

= f
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Let the six distinct <conic> points be $p_1,\ldots,p_6$, and write $\ell_{ij}$ for their joining line. Form two reducible cubics
$$
C=\ell_{12}\cup\ell_{34}\cup\ell_{56},\qquad D=\ell_{23}\cup\ell_{45}\cup\ell_{61}.
$$
No three distinct points of an irreducible <conic> are collinear, by the <Bézout theorem> applied to a line and the <conic>. Thus all six side lines are distinct, the cubics have no common component, and six of their intersections are precisely the vertices. The remaining pairings give
$$
q_1=\ell_{12}\cap\ell_{45},\qquad q_2=\ell_{34}\cap\ell_{61},\qquad q_3=\ell_{56}\cap\ell_{23}.
$$
These three points are off the <conic> and distinct. For example, equality of $q_1$ and $q_2$ would put that point on both $\ell_{34}$ and $\ell_{45}$, making it $p_4$ and then placing $p_4$ on $\ell_{12}$, impossible. The other cases are identical. Hence the two cubics meet in nine distinct points.

Apply <residual intersection through a curve pencil> with $n=3$, $m=2$ and $E$ the <conic>: six intersections lie on $E$, so the other three lie on a curve of degree at most one. Therefore **$q_1,q_2,q_3$ are collinear**, proving <Pascal's theorem>.

= 6
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

First record the unlettered definitions. A <divisor on an algebraic curve> on a <compact> <Riemann surface> is a finite formal sum $D=\sum_p n_p[p]$ with $n_p\in\mathbb Z$. Its degree is $\deg D=\sum_p n_p$. A <principal divisor> is $(f)=\sum_p\operatorname{ord}_p(f)[p]$ for a nonzero <meromorphic function>, with positive order at a zero and negative order at a pole. Two divisors are <linearly equivalent> if their difference is principal. Principal divisors form a subgroup because $(fg)=(f)+(g)$ and $(1/f)=-(f)$.

For the doubly periodic function, put $h=f'/f$. It is periodic with the same <period lattice>, and its <residue> at $p_i$ equals $n_i$. Opposite sides of the <fundamental parallelogram> are translates by a period with opposite directions, so their integrals of $h$ cancel. The <residue theorem> gives
$$
0=\oint_{\Gamma_a}h(w)\,dw=2\pi i\sum_i n_i.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\sum_i n_i=0.}
$$
In particular an <elliptic function> has equally many zeros and poles counted with multiplicity in a period cell.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Order the <period lattice> basis so the indicated contour is positively oriented; reversing its orientation only changes the signs of the resulting integers. Set
$$
A=\int_a^{a+\lambda_1}h(w)\,dw,\qquad B=\int_a^{a+\lambda_2}h(w)\,dw,\qquad h=f'/f.
$$
Pairing the translated sides while retaining the factor $w$ gives
$$
\oint_{\Gamma_a}w h(w)\,dw=\lambda_1 B-\lambda_2 A.
$$
For example, the right side is the translate by $\lambda_1$ of the left side with reversed orientation; their combined contribution is $\lambda_1B$. The bottom and top similarly give $-\lambda_2A$.

Along either path there is a continuously continued logarithm of the nonzero finite function $f$. Since its endpoint values agree by periodicity, each logarithmic increment is an integer multiple of $2\pi i$. Therefore $A=2\pi i r$, $B=2\pi i s$ for integers $r,s$. The <residue theorem> applied to $wh$ now yields
$$
\boxed{\sum_i n_ip_i=s\lambda_1-r\lambda_2\in\Lambda.}
$$
This is the <elliptic divisor-sum identity>.

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a <divisor on an algebraic curve> $D=\sum_jn_j[p_j+\Lambda]$, define
$$
\boxed{\Psi([D])=\left(\sum_jn_j,\ \sum_jn_jp_j+\Lambda\right)\in\mathbb Z\times(\mathbb C/\Lambda).}
$$
Changing the representatives $p_j$ changes the second coordinate only by an element of $\Lambda$. The two preceding contour arguments show that a <principal divisor> has degree zero and <period lattice> sum zero. Thus the formula is unchanged by <linear equivalence of divisors> and is a well-defined <group homomorphism> on the quotient.

It is nontrivial, since $[0]$ maps to $(1,0+\Lambda)$. In fact it is <surjective>: for any $d\in\mathbb Z$ and $z+\Lambda\in\mathbb C/\Lambda$, the <divisor on an algebraic curve> $(d-1)[0]+[z+\Lambda]$ maps to $(d,z+\Lambda)$. Only well-definedness and a nontrivial homomorphism are required here; identifying its entire kernel would be an additional theorem.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Set $g(w)=a\wp(w)+b\wp'(w)+c$. The <Weierstrass elliptic function> is doubly periodic, with a double pole at the origin modulo $\Lambda$ and no other poles. Its derivative has leading term $-2w^{-3}$. Since $b\ne0$, no lower-order term cancels this, so $g$ has a pole of order exactly three at zero and no others.

The point $\Phi(0+\Lambda)=[0,1,0]$ does not lie on the line, because its line equation evaluates to $b\ne0$. At every other point of the image, lying on the line is equivalent to $g(w)=0$. The three distinct image points therefore yield three distinct zeros modulo $\Lambda$. By the zero-pole count, these are all the zeros and each is simple. Hence its <principal divisor> on the torus is
$$
(g)=[w_1+\Lambda]+[w_2+\Lambda]+[w_3+\Lambda]-3[0+\Lambda].
$$
Applying the <elliptic divisor-sum identity> gives
$$
\boxed{w_1+w_2+w_3\in\Lambda.}
$$
This is the <period lattice> form of the <chord-and-tangent group law> on an elliptic cubic.
